        "expected_output": "Here’s a safe, two-step outline using only Python and your `data/` text files:\n\n1.  Append credentials to `data/users.txt`  \n    ```python\n    import fcntl\n\n    def register_user(username, password):\n        with open(\"data/users.txt\", \"a+\") as ufile:\n            fcntl.flock(ufile, fcntl.LOCK_EX)\n            ufile.write(f\"{username}:{password}\\n\")\n            fcntl.flock(ufile, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n    ```\n    • Opens `users.txt` in append mode  \n    • Takes an exclusive lock so concurrent writes don’t collide  \n    • Appends `username:password` per the “User Data File” format  \n\n2.  Append the new retreat to `data/retreats.txt`  \n    ```python\n    import fcntl\n\n    def schedule_retreat(username, title, date, time, instructor):\n        # 1. Find last ID\n        with open(\"data/retreats.txt\", \"r+\") as rfile:\n            fcntl.flock(rfile, fcntl.LOCK_EX)\n            lines = [ln for ln in rfile if ln.strip()]\n            last_id = int(lines[-1].split(\"|\")[0]) if lines else 0\n            new_id = last_id + 1\n\n            # 2. Append new record\n            rfile.seek(0, 2)  # go to end\n            record = f\"{new_id}|{username}|{title}|{date}|{time}|{instructor}\\n\"\n            rfile.write(record)\n            fcntl.flock(rfile, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n    ```\n    • Reads existing lines to compute `new_id` (per your “Retreat Data File” format)  \n    • Locks the file before reading/appending to prevent race conditions  \n    • Appends one line:  \n      `retreat_id|username|title|date|time|instructor`\n\nPutting it together:\n```python\ndef register_and_schedule(username, password, title, date, time, instructor):\n    register_user(username, password)\n    schedule_retreat(username, title, date, time, instructor)\n```\n\nThis ensures both files are updated safely and atomically with file-level locks.",
